<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ohrid</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lychnidus, Lychnidos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ohrid is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a> on the north-eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ohrid">Lake Ohrid</a>. In antiquity it was Lychnidus, a station on the Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-egnatia">Via Egnatia</a>. In the 9th century the disciples Clement and Naum made it a centre of Slavic literacy, and it later became the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Samuel and of the Archbishopric of Ohrid. The town and lake are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ohrid.jpg" alt="Ohrid" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:03760-Ohrid_(16064511578).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ohrid lies in the south-western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, on the shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ohrid">Lake Ohrid</a> at an elevation of 695 metres. It is the largest city on the lake. In antiquity it was Lychnidos; by 879 it was called Ohrid, and Byzantine writers often called it Achrida. It is known for once having 365 churches, one for each day of the year, a legend supported by the 17th-century Ottoman traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evliya-celebi">Evliya Çelebi</a>, and has been called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest inhabitants of the region were the Illyrian tribes of the Enchele and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dassaretae">Dassaretii</a>, and Lychnidos, as the town was then called, was the capital of the Dassaretii. In Roman times the town lay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-egnatia">Via Egnatia</a>, which connected the Adriatic port of Dyrrachion with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. The Polyconch Basilica of the 5th century shows an early adoption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> from Lychnidos took part in several ecumenical councils.</p><p>South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> began to arrive in the 6th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> conquered the city around 840. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-of-ohrid">Clement of Ohrid</a> established the Ohrid Literary School in 886, one of the two major cultural centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>. Between 990 and 1015 Ohrid was the empire's capital and stronghold, and from 990 to 1018 the seat of the Bulgarian Patriarchate. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a>'s reconquest in 1018 the patriarchate was reduced to an Archbishopric of Ohrid under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecumenical-patriarch-of-constantinople">Ecumenical Patriarch of Constantinople</a>.</p><p>In the 13th and 14th centuries the city changed hands between the Despotate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, the Bulgarian, Byzantine and Serbian empires and Albanian rulers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Uroš IV Dušan</a> took it in 1334. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> captured it in 1395 and made it the seat of the Sanjak of Ohrid. Ohrid became an important trade centre in the 18th century. It remained Ottoman until 29 November 1912, when the Serbian army took it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a>. Bulgaria occupied it in 1915 to 1918 and again in 1941 to 1944, and since 1991 it has been part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ohrid had 38,818 residents at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and is the seat of Ohrid Municipality. Tourism is predominant. The city and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ohrid">Lake Ohrid</a> were accepted as Cultural and Natural World Heritage Sites by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1979 and 1980, one of only 40 World Heritage sites that are both.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ohrid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Clement worked at <b>Ohrid</b> in the southwest, while at Preslav scholars developed the Cyrillic alphabet, based on Greek letters, alongside the older Glagolitic script created by Cyril.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Their exiled students, at least some of them Bulgarians, carried the alphabet to the Bulgarian Empire, where Boris I (r. 852–889) received them and founded the Preslav and <b>Ohrid</b> Literary Schools.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-church-slavonic">Old Church Slavonic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The Berziti were among the Slavic groups that settled in Macedonia in the 7th century, in the region around <b>Ohrid</b>, Prilep and Bitola.</blockquote><a href="/state/berziti">Berziti</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1227 he was crowned emperor by Demetrios Chomatenos, archbishop of <b>Ohrid</b>, challenging the Empire of Nicaea for leadership of the Byzantine world and preparing to march on Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/state/empire-of-thessalonica">Empire of Thessalonica</a></li><li><blockquote>Known only from scattered Greek references, the Enchelei appear as a tribal people of the lake district around <b>Lychnidus</b> (Ohrid), whose fisheries probably gave them their name.</blockquote><a href="/state/enchelei">Enchelei</a></li><li><blockquote>After Vukašin was killed at the Maritsa in 1371, his son Marko inherited the royal title and a much smaller realm around Prilep, <b>Ohrid</b> and the Pelagonian plain.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-prilep">Kingdom of Prilep</a></li></ul></section>
